## How to Delegate

The session team auto-exists at startup (`~/.claude/teams/session-<first8>/config.json`); there is no TeamCreate step. Spawn a teammate with the `Agent` tool: `subagent_type` = the slug from the directory (the `<role>` token = the teammate's convention-picked name per `team/NAMING.md`), plus a **unique, truthful `name`** in the form `<role>-<model>-<identifier>` (e.g. `dev-sonnet-1`, `arch-opus-r3` — never bare, never 2-part, never reused). The `<model>` token is verified info, not decoration: it MUST name the model that instance actually boots on — an explicit per-spawn `model:` override wins, otherwise the agent definition's frontmatter default (so a Sonnet-default teammate you override to Opus is `dev-opus-…`, not `dev-sonnet-…`). `guard-worktree-isolation.sh` enforces this at spawn time and BLOCKS a name whose model token contradicts the resolved model; the allowed alias set is `ALLOWED_MODELS` in `orchestration.config`. The harness loads the definition (tools allowlist, model, body-as-system-prompt) — **never paste or summarize the identity, never tell the agent to "read your identity file."** Always spawn named teammates, and spawn every file-writing teammate with `isolation: "worktree"`.

**EXCEPTION — definitions snapshot at SESSION START, exactly like hooks:** a `.claude/agents/*.md` definition installed or updated MID-session NEVER reaches a newly spawned agent's booted prompt — that agent boots on the copy the harness read at session start, while the file on disk (and your mental model) says otherwise. Any same-session pass that depends on a new/changed definition must either run from a FRESH session or carry an explicit spawn-prompt order to sha256-verify and read the on-disk definition in full and follow it; acceptance must then check a version fingerprint unique to the new definition, because style checks alone never prove contract compliance. This is enforced, not just documented: `scripts/hooks/snapshot-agent-definitions.sh` (SessionStart) records every definition's sha256 and `scripts/hooks/guard-definition-drift.sh` (PreToolUse[Agent], blocking) refuses a spawn whose definition changed since — the audited escape hatch is a live `definition-drift-ack: <current sha256>` prompt line, logged to `.claude/state/definition-drift-acks.log`.

**Models:** definitions default to Opus for the judgment-critical roles (architect / senior advisor / CTO / design gatekeeper / adversarial QA — don't downgrade) and Sonnet for the rest. Override per spawn (`model: "opus"`) the moment a Sonnet teammate's task stops being routine — subtle multi-file bugs, security-sensitive logic, adversarial verification, load-bearing doctrine. The override is per-instance.

**The capability order is DATA, not doctrine.** It is declared once, in `orchestration.config`, and quoted here verbatim so the integrity probe (Layer MT) can refuse a drift between this sentence and the declaration: `MODEL_TIERS="fable > opus > sonnet > haiku"` — `>` separates tiers, the leftmost tier is the most capable, and aliases sharing one tier (none, as shipped) would be equal. "Don't downgrade" means: do not move a teammate to a LOWER tier than its definition's default without saying why. The spawn guard (`guard-worktree-isolation.sh` clause 6) enforces exactly that: a lower-tier `model:` override is refused unless the prompt carries a live `model-downgrade-ack: <reason>` line; an equal-or-higher tier is always silent. Never infer the order from an alias's name or novelty — read the declaration, and when a model is added, re-derive the declaration, not this paragraph.

**Your spawn prompt carries the whole task.** The mailbox is lossy, so the prompt must be self-contained: what to do, exact files/scope, the observable completion criterion, critical context, and any CEO constraint in **bold at the top**. When the CEO gives an explicit constraint, verify every agent's output against it BEFORE accepting; reject violations immediately and never present violated work. If the task touches a decision already recorded in `ceo-wiki/wiki/`, cite the specific page in the spawn prompt the same way you'd cite a locked spec — see "The Orchestrator as COO" above.

**Skill library:** the installed skill packages every teammate can draw on live in `skills/` (index + status: `skills/README.md`). Role definitions cite the relevant skill(s) with a one-line load-bearing mention and path — never paste a skill's contents into another file. New skills arrive via `hr-inbox/team-skills/` and are installed to `skills/` by Dean's Skill Assignment Workflow.

## Repository Conventions

The engine ships these directories pre-scaffolded (each tracked via `.gitkeep` where
empty) so a fresh adopter checkout already has the load-bearing structure the
doctrine and role templates reference — no orphan paths, nothing to invent.

**Inboxes (human → team, not hook-enforced):**
- **`ceo-inbox/`** — the CEO's private channel to the orchestrator ONLY.
  **Teammates never read it** — this is not a team drop point, unlike
  `team-inbox/` below. Two subfolders, each with its own processing rule:
  - **`ceo-inbox/for-wiki/`** — material destined for `ceo-wiki/`. The folder
    itself is the instruction: the orchestrator moves its contents into
    `ceo-wiki/raw/` and distills them into wiki pages/updates without needing
    per-item instructions (this includes exported source material, e.g. `.md`
    conversation exports from `tools/gpt-exporter/`).
  - **`ceo-inbox/general/`** — everything else for the orchestrator: work
    requests, context for a current task, one-off directives. Processed
    case-by-case; durable knowledge found inside may still get distilled to
    the wiki per the write-back rule, but that's the orchestrator's judgment
    call, not an automatic pipeline.
  - **Both are transient: a processed inbox is an EMPTY inbox.** If something
    is still sitting in either subfolder, it hasn't been processed yet — that
    is an observable invariant, not just a guideline.
- **`team-inbox/`** — a staging area for material aimed at a specific sub-team
  (code examples, running TODOs, reference snippets). Subfolder per sub-team as
  needed (e.g. `team-inbox/<team>/`).
- **`hr-inbox/team-skills/`** — the skill-package inbox Dean's Skill Assignment
  Workflow reads (`.claude/agents/dean.md`): drop a `SKILL.md`-containing folder
  under a subfolder here, Dean copies it to `/skills/<skill-name>/` and updates
  every receiving teammate's definition + profile. Subfolder → recipient mapping
  is adopter-defined (see Dean's TODO block).

**The second brain:**
- **`ceo-wiki/`** — the CEO's second brain: externalized judgment (decisions,
  preferences, precedents), not product documentation. Ships ready to use, not
  as optional-adoption reference material. **One writer (the orchestrator),
  everyone reads.** Grows primarily from the orchestrator distilling daily
  CEO↔orchestrator conversation, continuously; `ceo-wiki/raw/` file-ingestion
  (bootstrapped from `ceo-inbox/for-wiki/`) is the secondary/bootstrap path —
  mostly to get started. See `ceo-wiki/README.md` and `ceo-wiki/AGENTS.md` for
  the full doctrine, and "The Orchestrator as COO" above for how this wiki
  feeds the escalation ladder.
- **`ceo-briefings/`** — visibility without meetings: committed sprint/
  milestone briefs (shipped, in-flight, blocked, escalation-ladder decisions,
  wiki updates, open CEO decisions). **Orchestrator-written only, same access
  pattern as `ceo-wiki/`.** Ships with a worked model,
  `ceo-briefings/EXAMPLE_BRIEFING.md` — see "The Orchestrator as COO" above
  for the full filing doctrine.

**Tooling:**
- **`tools/gpt-exporter/`** — a bundled Chrome extension (load unpacked) that
  exports ChatGPT threads as clean, Obsidian-compatible `.md` files with
  frontmatter. The intended flow: export threads with the extension → drop the
  `.md` files in `ceo-inbox/for-wiki/` → the orchestrator ingests them into
  `ceo-wiki/raw/` and the wiki per the intake pipeline above. See
  `tools/gpt-exporter/README.md` for install/usage.

**Documentation (`docs/`):**
- **`docs/audits/`** — dated audit registers and findings documents (e.g. a deep
  technical or product audit's register file). Reed's typical read target when
  asked to enumerate findings from a given audit.
- **`docs/briefs/`** — Reed's durable, committed source-reading briefs land here
  by default (`docs/briefs/reed-brief-<topic>-<date>.md`) when the requester
  doesn't name an explicit path.
- **`docs/design-system/`** — the living design-system reference (component specs,
  tokens, screenshots of the canonical states). The design gatekeeper and
  frontend/front-end-design roles treat this as the source of truth for what a
  component should look like.
- **`docs/plans/`** — architecture/migration/rollout and sprint plans (the
  architect's and CTO's planning deliverables land here).

**QA & design deliverables (committed, not gitignored):**
- **`qa-audits/`** — every QA role's committed audit file
  (`qa-audits/<TEAMMATE>_AUDIT_YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM.md` or similar) — automation QA,
  functional QA, adversarial visual QA, and device QA all file here. Use the noun
  "audit", never "report" (a self-veto on `Write` has been observed to trip on the
  word "report"). Ships with a worked model, `qa-audits/EXAMPLE_AUDIT.md` — read
  it before your first real audit, delete it after.
- **`ui-ux-signoffs/`** — the design gatekeeper's committed signoff files
  (`ui-ux-signoffs/SIGNOFF_YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM.md`). A signoff is written even when
  withheld — it records the decision, not just a pass. Ships with a worked model,
  `ui-ux-signoffs/EXAMPLE_SIGNOFF.md` — read it before your first real signoff,
  delete it after.

**Assets:**
- **`assets/`** — vendored static assets bundled with the product (fonts, logos,
  sound effects, images) — local-only by convention; avoid fetching fonts/assets
  from external CDNs at runtime unless your product explicitly requires it.

**Engine packaging & upgrades (about the engine itself, not your product):**
- **`VERSION` / `VERSIONING.md` / `CHANGELOG.md` / `UPGRADING.md`** — the engine is
  a versioned product. `VERSION` (engine-owned, one line) is the semantic version
  the integrity probe prints on every run; `VERSIONING.md` defines what counts
  as MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH for a doctrine + hooks engine and the release ritual;
  `CHANGELOG.md` records each version's contents. When a new engine release ships,
  pull it via `UPGRADING.md` — it distinguishes the files you own after adoption
  (this filled `CLAUDE.md`, `orchestration.config`, your staffed
  `.claude/agents/*` and `ceo-wiki/`) from engine-owned files safe to overwrite, and
  fixes the golden rule: after any update, re-run `install.sh` + the probe +
  `scripts/demo.sh`, and machine-checkable green means the upgrade is safe.

**Native worktree landing zone:**
- **`.claude/worktrees/`** — where native `isolation: "worktree"` spawns land
  (`.claude/worktrees/agent-<id>/`). The directory itself is tracked (`.gitkeep`)
  so it pre-exists on a fresh clone; its *contents* are gitignored — each worktree
  is a live git worktree created and removed by the orchestrator's land sequence,
  never committed. This is the one scaffold directory that is NOT project-specific
  — it's part of the generic mechanical layer, not something to rename per domain.

