## Hard Rules — NEVER violate these

### Session Configuration — NEVER remove these two `.claude/settings.local.json` keys

**`env.CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS: "1"` and `worktree.baseRef: "head"` are as load-bearing as any hook.** The env flag was once accidentally deleted during an edit, and at the NEXT session start the orchestrator could not see or spawn ANY teammates — a silent total failure with no error shown. `worktree.baseRef` is what makes every native isolation worktree branch from local HEAD, which the whole worktree/land doctrine below assumes. `scripts/hooks/contract-integrity-probe.sh` hard-fails (Layers I/J) if either drifts — if teammates ever go missing, run it before anything else. `scripts/demo.sh` is the broader end-to-end check (README's "60-second proof") — it exercises the whole hook chain plus real worktree/merge mechanics against a throwaway sample, not just the wiring layers the probe checks.

**Hooks register in exactly ONE settings file — the committed `.claude/settings.local.json`.** Claude Code reads it directly, so enforcement is live on clone with no wiring step. It must NOT also be copied into a generated `.claude/settings.json`: Claude Code reads BOTH files and MERGES their hook arrays additively, so a second copy fires every hook TWICE per matching tool event (silent for the idempotent guards; visible only as byte-identical duplicate lines in the append-loggers). `scripts/hooks/install.sh` no longer writes hook stanzas — it only mints the gitignored `.sha256` sidecars and migrates away any stale hook-duplicating `settings.json`; the probe's Layer M asserts single-registration uniqueness and fails the double-fire.

